'''Jukun''' or '''Djugun''' is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] of [[Western Australia]]. There are no longer any fluent speakers of Jukun, but some people may remember it to some degree. It is an Eastern Nyulnyulan language, closely related to [[Yawuru language|Yawuru]].<ref>{{e25|dyd}}</ref>

== Revitalization ==
A book titled ''50 Words in Djukun: A Language Of The West Kimberley'' was published in 2025, facilitating the revitalization of the Djukun language.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Davidson |first=Jamie |date=2025-12-12 |title=50 words in Djukun - A language of the West Kimberley |url=https://arts.unimelb.edu.au/research-unit-for-indigenous-language/news-and-events/50-words-in-djukun |access-date=2026-04-28 |website=Faculty of Arts |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Advertiser |first=Cain AndrewsBroome |date=2026-01-29 |title=New Broome children’s book helping preserve Djukun language |url=https://www.broomead.com.au/news/broome-advertiser/new-broome-childrens-book-helping-preserve-djukun-language--c-21354200 |access-date=2026-04-28 |website=Broome Advertiser |language=en}}</ref>

